abstract: >-
  Flow regimes play a fundamental role in shaping river geomorphology, ecological
  processes, and aquatic community structure. However, climate change and human
  interventions are increasingly modifying natural hydrological regimes, leading to
  altered flow patterns, biodiversity loss, water scarcity, and disruption of
  hydro-ecological conditions. Hydrological models provide a robust framework for
  reconstructing natural conditions and assessing hydrologic alteration under
  different scenarios. In this context, SWAT+ (Soil and Water Assessment Tool) is a
  widely used process-based hydrological model capable of simulating daily streamflow
  across complex river basins, while IAHRIS (Indicators of Hydrologic Alteration in
  RIverS) provides an indicator-based framework to characterize natural and altered
  flow regimes. Nevertheless, coupling SWAT+ outputs with IAHRIS remains a
  time-consuming and error-prone task due to differences in data structures and
  preprocessing requirements. This study presents SWAT+IAHRIS, an open-source software
  tool developed in Python to automate the post-processing of SWAT+ daily streamflow
  outputs for hydrologic alteration assessment using the IAHRIS framework. The tool is
  directly integrated into the SWAT+ environment and automatically generates seven
  structured Excel reports covering natural and altered flow characterization,
  natural-altered regime comparison, flow-duration curves, habitual-flow indicators,
  floods, droughts, and environmental significance. The applicability of SWAT+IAHRIS is
  demonstrated through two representative case studies in the Sorbe River basin,
  central Spain: the assessment of dam-induced flow alteration downstream of the Beleña
  reservoir and the evaluation of long-term climate-change impacts on river flow
  regimes. Results demonstrate that SWAT+IAHRIS provides an efficient, reproducible,
  and user-friendly bridge between process-based hydrological modelling and
  indicator-based hydrologic alteration diagnosis, supporting standardized assessments
  of hydrologic alteration in river systems affected by climate change and human
  regulation.
